WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.400
Imagine waking up tomorrow and
nobody in India could speak English.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.370
Think about courts, universities,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.270
international businesses
and millions of workplaces.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.450
English is deeply embedded
in many of these systems.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:12.640
Government communication,

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.440
legal documents and technical
education would need major adjustments.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.040
Companies working with international
clients would have to rethink how they

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:20.570
communicate.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.690
But India wouldn't become silent.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.300
We have hundreds of languages,
each with its own history and identity.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.610
The real challenge would be replacing one
common communication bridge across so many

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:31.660
different communities.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.670
Would India become more united
through its regional languages,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.530
or would communication become harder?

